== Transport-layer analysis (NIC 511, 2026) ==

The following analysis was performed passively against a residential
'''Aclara I-210+c''' electric meter carrying a '''Silver Spring Networks
NIC 511''' radio module (FCC ID OWS-NIC511), on the 902–928 MHz band, using an
RTL-SDR. It recovers the '''transport layer''' of the mesh — framing, data
whitening, integrity check, addressing, channel plan and the frequency-hopping
sequence. The application payload is AES-CCM* encrypted and is '''not'''
recovered. Reproducible code and a full write-up:
[https://github.com/bash-tilde/silverspring-ami-rf silverspring-ami-rf].

All node identifiers below are shown as illustrative placeholders; no real meter
identifiers are published.

'''Relationship to the existing page:''' the modulation modes above are from the
FCC filing of an earlier meter (SK9AMI7). The measurements here are from a
different, later module (NIC 511) and differ in places — most notably the
channel plan (see below). Where they differ, treat them as distinct device
generations rather than corrections.

=== On-air variants (read first) ===

"Silver Spring Networks protocol" is not a single on-air format. At least three
distinct data points now exist, and they do '''not''' share framing or scrambler:

* '''This page's capture''' (meter <code>…008C900A</code>): post-preamble
  <code>de 9d 27 27 16 66 f0 6c</code>. Matches neither of the two below.
* '''rtl_433 <code>silver_spring_mesh</code>''' (Benjamin Larsson): a
  drive-by/water-meter variant (Badger) — <code>SFD 0xF3A0</code>, 3-byte PHR,
  '''8-bit''' scrambler (<code>x⁸+x⁴+x³+x²+1</code>), 4-byte CRC-32/MPEG-2 FCS.
* '''This analysis''' (Aclara I-210+c / NIC 511, fixed AMI): sync
  <code>0C 5F &lt;ch&gt; FF</code>, 2-byte inverted PHR, '''degree-9''' LFSR
  whitening, 1-byte CRC-32-derived trailer.

The two scramblers are provably different, not two descriptions of one: the
8-bit scrambler descrambles none of the NIC 511 frames under any of its 255
seeds, while Berlekamp–Massey measures the NIC 511 keystream's linear complexity
as 9 — which an 8-bit LFSR cannot generate. Sections below document the NIC 511
fixed-AMI variant; see rtl_433 for the drive-by/water one.

=== Physical layer ===

2-GFSK, modulation index ≈ 0.5, at 100/150/200 kBd (varying per frame). Bursts
were extracted from raw IQ by envelope energy detection, then demodulated by
frequency discrimination and symbol-timing recovery.

=== Frame / PHY header — the channel is transmitted in the clear ===

What appears as a long <code>aa aa…</code>/<code>55 55…</code> preamble is
followed by a 4-byte PHY header, '''not''' a fixed sync word:

 0C 5F <X> FF

Bytes 0, 1 and 3 are constant (<code>0C 5F … FF</code>); '''byte 2 is the channel
number''', encoded as <code>N = 255 − X</code>. This differs from the earlier
page's <code>0xAAAAAAAA</code> sync guess, which is the preamble continuation.

=== Channel plan ===

Fitting the measured per-frame carrier against the header channel number N:

 f(N) = 902.2990 MHz + 299.991 kHz · N        N = 0 … 86   (87 channels)

Residuals are 0–2 kHz on a 300 kHz grid for every channel the receiver could
hear. '''Note the discrepancy''' with the FCC-documented 64-channel / 400 kHz
mode: on this NIC 511 unit the observed plan is 87 channels at 300 kHz spacing.
This may reflect a different device configuration or firmware generation, not an
error in the FCC data.

=== Data whitening — degree-9 LFSR ===

The PSDU is whitened. Long frames carry a run of all-zero payload padding, so the
ciphertext there is the raw keystream. Berlekamp–Massey on ~192 bits of it
returns '''linear complexity 9''' (a random stream would give ~96), recovering
the generator directly with no search:

 b[n] = b[n−1] ⊕ b[n−2] ⊕ b[n−5] ⊕ b[n−8] ⊕ b[n−9]        period 255

Because plaintext bytes 0–4 are the constant serial prefix
<code>00 13 50 05 00</code>, the mask for ''any'' frame is recoverable from that
frame alone (<code>mask[0:5] = cipher[0:5] ⊕ prefix</code> seeds the 9-bit
register). 100% of captured frames de-whiten to a valid serial prefix — a
2<sup>−40</sup> per-frame coincidence probability.

=== Integrity — CRC-32 ===

The trailing byte is the top byte of a '''CRC-32''' (polynomial
<code>0x04C11DB7</code>, MSB-first, init 0) over the de-whitened frame, XORed
with a per-length constant K[len]:

 K = {9:0x78, 12:0x0E, 14:0x5B, 16:0x7A, 21:0xEB, 22:0xE8, 29:0x05,
      55:0xA6, 111:0x7D, 112:0x2D, 134:0x6E, 213:0x60}

It validates on 386/392 captured frames; the 6 failures are independently
corroborated corrupt frames. This directly addresses the earlier page's note
that "no checking is done of the packets" — a working per-frame integrity check
now exists.

=== Framing grammar ===

The length field determines the header stack deterministically. This grammar
accounts for every byte of all observed length classes:

 frame := SRC(8) [DST(8)] {headers} payload TRAILER(1)

 ln= 12   SRC | 04 81 xx | TRAILER
 ln= 14   SRC | 01 03 aa bb cc | TRAILER
 ln= 22   SRC | DST | 01 03 aa bb cc | TRAILER
 ln= 29   SRC | DST | 01 03 .. | PAYLOAD(7) | TRAILER
 ln= 55   SRC | 01 03 .. | 04 81 xx | PAYLOAD(38) | TRAILER
 ln=111   SRC | 01 80 | 00 82 .. | PAYLOAD(96) | TRAILER   (beacon)
 ln=213   SRC | 00 82 .. | 01 03 .. | PAYLOAD(195) | TRAILER  (encrypted)

Node addresses are 8 bytes with OUI <code>00:13:50</code> (Silver Spring).
Endpoints use a serial format (<code>00:13:50:05:00:xx:xx:xx</code>);
infrastructure/access points use an EUI-64 form
(<code>00:13:50:FF:FE:60:xx:xx:xx</code>, a MAC-48 → EUI-64 mapping).

=== Flags byte ===

The PHR's second byte decomposes as <code>fl = base(channel) ⊕ code</code>,
where code ∈ {0 = broadcast, 2 = unicast, 3 = beacon}. Zero exceptions across
445 frames.

=== Beacon timing and frequency hopping ===

One node type (a local relay) beacons on an '''exact 120.000 s lattice''' (phase
σ = 0.25 s over 450+ cycles). Assigning each beacon an integer slot index, the
channel is a function of '''slot mod 83''': uniquely at N = 83, with 153/153
strictly causal forward predictions and a search-corrected permutation null of
0/3000. Cycle length ≈ 2 h 46 min.

=== Network topology ===

The meter studied is a dual-homed '''relay''': across 227 beacons over 68 h it
advertised a fixed route to two EUI-64 access points, one with a stable link
metric (σ = 8) and one volatile (σ = 86). ~60 endpoint meters route through it.
The access points are never heard on air — they are the root of the local tree,
on unmonitored channels, known only by name from the relay's beacon.

=== Application payload — encrypted ===

The <code>ln=213</code> payload (195 bytes) is uniform-entropy with no zero
bytes. Two such frames from the same source, captured 22 h apart, '''XOR to
full-entropy noise''' (3/195 identical bytes), proving per-frame keystream
uniqueness — a fixed scrambler or keystream reuse is excluded. Combined with a
per-frame nonce field and incompressibility, this is '''AES-CCM* encryption'''
(consistent with Itron/Certicom NSA Suite B). The key is provisioned by the
head-end key manager and is not present on the air; the transport is open, the
payload is closed by design.

